1
00:00:00,279 --> 00:00:06,159
great deal, comrades. Did we not see for ourselves how he attempted, fortunately

2
00:00:06,159 --> 00:00:12,960
without success, to get us defeated and destroyed at the Battle of the Cowshed?

3
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,479
The animals were stupefied.

4
00:00:15,479 --> 00:00:20,520
This was a wickedness far outdoing Snowball's destruction of the windmill.

5
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:24,039
But it was some minutes before they could fully take it in.

6
00:00:24,039 --> 00:00:28,880
They all remembered, or thought they remembered, how they had seen Snowball

7
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:33,159
charging ahead of them at the Battle of the Cowshed, how he had rallied and

8
00:00:33,159 --> 00:00:39,399
encouraged them at every turn, and how he had not paused for an instant even

9
00:00:39,399 --> 00:00:44,199
when the pellets from Jones's gun had wounded his back.

10
00:00:44,199 --> 00:00:47,920
At first, it was a little difficult to see how this fitted in with his being on

11
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:49,880
Jones's side.

12
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:54,720
Even Boxer, who seldom asked questions, was puzzled.

13
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:59,600
He lay down, tucked his four hooves beneath him, shut his eyes, and with a

14
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:03,760
hard effort managed to formulate his thoughts.

15
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:05,920
>> I do not believe that.

16
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:10,319
Snowball fought bravely at the Battle of the Cowshed. I saw him myself.

17
00:01:10,319 --> 00:01:15,439
Did we not give him Animal Hero First Class immediately afterwards?

18
00:01:15,439 --> 00:01:20,319
>> That was our mistake, comrade, for we know now, it is all written down in the

19
00:01:20,319 --> 00:01:27,359
secret documents that we have found, that in reality, he was trying to lure us to our doom.

20
00:01:27,359 --> 00:01:29,439
>> But he was wounded.

21
00:01:29,439 --> 00:01:33,439
We all saw him running with blood.

22
00:01:33,439 --> 00:01:35,880
>> That was part of the arrangement.

23
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:40,199
Jones's shot only grazed him. I could show you this in his own writing if you

24
00:01:40,199 --> 00:01:45,319
were able to read it. The plot was for Snowball at the critical moment to give

25
00:01:45,319 --> 00:01:50,119
the signal for flight and leave the field to the enemy, and he very nearly

26
00:01:50,119 --> 00:01:55,760
succeeded. I will even say, comrades, he would have succeeded if it had not been

27
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:59,399
for our heroic leader, comrade Napoleon.

28
00:01:59,399 --> 00:02:03,399
Do you not remember how, just at the moment when Jones and his men had got

29
00:02:03,399 --> 00:02:08,560
inside the yard, Snowball suddenly turned and fled, and many animals

30
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:13,439
followed him? And do you not remember too that it was just at that moment when

31
00:02:13,439 --> 00:02:19,079
panic was spreading and all seemed lost that comrade Napoleon sprang forward

32
00:02:19,079 --> 00:02:25,760
with a cry of death to humanity and sank his teeth in Jones's leg. Surely you

33
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:28,119
remember that comrades.

34
00:02:28,119 --> 00:02:31,880
>> Exclaimed Squealer frisking from side to side.

35
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:36,680
Now when Squealer described the scenes so graphically, it seemed to the animals

36
00:02:36,680 --> 00:02:39,039
that they did remember it.

37
00:02:39,039 --> 00:02:43,600
At any rate, they remembered that at the critical moment of the battle, Snowball

38
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,960
had turned to flee.

39
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:49,319
But Boxer was still a little uneasy.

40
00:02:49,319 --> 00:02:53,560
>> I do not believe that Snowball was a traitor at the beginning.

41
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:55,399
He said finally.

42
00:02:55,399 --> 00:02:58,600
What he has done since is different.

43
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:04,920
But I believe that at the Battle of the Cowshed, he was a good comrade.

44
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:12,720
>> Our leader, comrade Napoleon, >> announced Squealer speaking very slowly and firmly.

45
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:18,680
Has stated categorically, categorically comrade, that Snowball was Jones's agent

46
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:24,600
from the very beginning. Yes, and from long before the rebellion was ever thought of.

47
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:27,720
>> Ah, that is different.

48
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:32,840
If comrade Napoleon says it, it must be right.

49
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:35,840
>> That is the true spirit comrade.

50
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:37,399
>> Cried Squealer.

51
00:03:37,399 --> 00:03:45,119
But it was noticed he cast a very ugly look at Boxer with his little twinkling eyes.

52
00:03:45,119 --> 00:03:49,800
He turned to go then paused and added impressively.

53
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,000
